00:01:41.860 --> 00:01:47.780
Okay, now for my favorite part of the show, where we slow down and see how one piece of this technology

00:01:47.780 --> 00:01:53.600
actually works. You know how your brain fills in the gaps between frames when you watch a short video

00:01:53.600 --> 00:02:00.580
on your phone, making motion look smooth. AI video tools now do something similar but across entire

00:02:00.580 --> 00:02:06.860
scenes instead of single frames. They study millions of clips to learn patterns about how objects move,

00:02:07.160 --> 00:02:12.940
how light shifts, and how one moment connects to the next. The research from Google DeepMind shows

00:02:12.940 --> 00:02:18.500
these tools can already judge depth in a scene or separate a main subject from the background,

00:02:18.920 --> 00:02:24.840
even without special training for those tasks. It happens because the system has built an internal

00:02:24.840 --> 00:02:30.460
picture of everyday physics, like knowing a ball will bounce when it hits the ground or that shadows

00:02:30.460 --> 00:02:35.840
follow the light source. That internal picture is what researchers call a world model,

00:02:36.200 --> 00:02:41.000
and it forms mostly from watching made-up videos yet still applies to real footage.

00:02:41.640 --> 00:02:45.240
So when an AI turns a text description into moving video,

00:02:45.560 --> 00:02:51.320
it is not just copying pixels from old clips. It is using everything it learned about real world

00:02:51.320 --> 00:02:56.820
behavior to fill in the missing parts and keep the motion believable. Reaching this point means

00:02:56.820 --> 00:03:02.140
the next time you see generated video, you can picture the system quietly running its own mental

00:03:02.140 --> 00:03:08.520
simulation of how things should act. Claude, the AI from Anthropic, now includes a dashboard

00:03:08.520 --> 00:03:14.240
